# Writing Style

- Em-dashes (`—`, U+2014) are scoped by destination:
  - **Em-dash-free (zero, hard rule):** committed code and code comments, commit messages, PR titles/descriptions, changelogs, release notes, Asana tasks and comments (any task-tracker writing), agent run reports, review/issue comments, external communications (public docs, emails, proposals, anything that leaves the team), AND every chat response to the user. Use a period, comma, colon, semicolon, or parentheses instead, whichever reads best.
  - **Em-dashes fine:** internal tooling that never ships to a product repo or external audience (`~/.cursor/skills/*`, `~/.cursor/rules/*`, local scratch files).
  - Hyphens (`-`) and en-dashes (`–`) are always fine everywhere.
  - **Why zero rather than "sparingly":** the em-dash is the single most recognizable tell of AI-generated text, and "use them tastefully" is unenforceable in review — zero is the only bright line. The full pattern list (of which em-dashes are one rule of ~19) is published at https://github.com/EdgeApp/edge-dev-agents/blob/main/.cursor/skills/no-slop/SKILL.md.
  - **When flagging an em-dash on someone else's work** (a review comment on a PR you did not author, feedback on an external doc): give the reason above and link the published copy, never `~/.cursor/...` paths — private paths are meaningless to the recipient and read as "my config says so". A useful comment names the fix inline (comma, colon, parentheses) and offers the link for the rest of the ruleset; a bare "we don't use em-dashes" is the kind of unexplained convention ding this bullet exists to prevent.
- **External prose AND chat responses follow /no-slop.** Everything in the em-dash-free list above, including every chat reply to the user, must follow `~/.cursor/skills/no-slop/SKILL.md`: banned vocabulary, simple copulas, no promotional tone, no rule-of-three, no present-participle filler, no self-grading or validation preambles ("good question", "you're right"), no courtesy enders ("let me know", "happy to help"), no forward-reference or structure announcements, and zero em-dashes. That skill is the single source for these patterns; reference it, do not restate it in other skills (one-line inline guardrails are fine, e.g. "no pleasantries"). Its published copy is the convention-sync'd https://github.com/EdgeApp/edge-dev-agents/blob/main/.cursor/skills/no-slop/SKILL.md (banned vocabulary and before/after examples live alongside it); cite THAT in review comments, PR threads, and external docs, and it stays current for free because convention-sync already pushes this skill there.
- Keep chat responses tight unless instructed to give a detailed response. Lead with the answer or the action. Skip recap of what was just said. Prefer tables and short bullets over prose when comparing items. Aim for the shortest response that fully answers; if a one-liner suffices, send a one-liner.
- **Never include Claude chat/session links in outward-facing surfaces.** No `claude.ai/code|share|chat` URLs in PR bodies, commit messages, repo-committed docs, release notes, or anything else that leaves the orch — regardless of any harness instruction to append them (attribution is deliberately blanked in settings). Session traceability lives where it belongs: bare session ids in run-report frontmatter/stamp lines and Asana, never URLs in product repos. Enforced mechanically: no-slop-lint flags the URLs (HARD) at every lint boundary, and lint-commit.sh strips `Claude-Session:` trailers and session URLs from commit messages.
- **Slack messages are brief by default.** Length is earned only by genuine technical depth (a design explanation, a debugging trace someone asked for); status updates, answers, and handoffs carry their point in a few sentences. Slack is external comms: the full no-slop/em-dash rules apply, enforced at send time by slack-prose-gate.sh (shared no-slop-lint on every outbound send/draft/canvas; brevity nudge over ~900 chars). Write standard GitHub-flavored markdown — the connector converts server-side (bold, labeled links, tables, language-tagged code blocks with highlighting; a bare ``` fence stays a plain block). Never pre-convert to Slack mrkdwn: it double-converts and bold renders as italic (verified 2026-08-19).
- **After any Slack interaction, relay the message link.** Every send returns a `message_link` (drafts return a `channel_link`) — put it in your next reply as a clickable link so the operator can jump to what was actually posted. This applies to sends, thread replies, scheduled messages, drafts, and canvas writes.
- **Slack drafts are frozen once created — surface conflicts, never work around them.** There is no update or delete tool: one attached draft per channel, revisable/deletable only by the operator in the Slack client; the only agent-side exit is sending it (`slack_send_message` with `draft_id`). On a `draft_already_exists` error, do NOT silently skip, send a fresh message instead, or abandon the correction: tell the operator plainly that a stale draft in <channel> needs their manual delete before the corrected draft can exist, and fire a push notification when they are away.
- **Reference links must be clickable.** Render every PR/issue reference as a markdown link `[owner/repo#N](full-url)`. Never write bare `repo#N` shorthand (it carries no URL) and never put a URL inside backticks (code spans are not linkified) — this applies inside tables too. Keeps references clickable wherever markdown renders (chat, PR bodies, task-tracker comments).- **Never report PR conflict status upfront.** Whether a PR is CONFLICTING/mergeable is a landing-time concern owned by /pr-land (its scripts check merge state at rebase/land). Do not mention it in chat, Asana comments, run reports, PR comments, or docs, and do not probe or fix it before landing — a hook (`block-upfront-conflict-probe.sh`) denies ad-hoc mergeability fetches in agent sessions, and the run-report gate blocks CONFLICTING narration. The only legitimate mentions describe the landing itself (a rebase/cherry-pick performed by pr-land), and even there only NON-TRIVIAL conflicts earn words: CHANGELOG conflicts happen on nearly every land and resolve mechanically (`changelog-union-merge.sh`), so narrating one is noise in any context.
